INT. PAWNSHOP INSIDE DEFUNCT AQUARIUM - NIGHT

A pawnshop built into the old shark tank. The glass walls are cracked and green with algae. Guitars hang where the hammerheads once circled. A neon sign buzzes: "CASH FOR ANYTHING."

SAL "THE EEL" DiMARCO, sixties, slick-haired, in a shiny shirt open to the chest, sits behind a counter counting bills. His smile never reaches his eyes.

FRANCESCA OYELOWO, thirties, a detective in a navy raincoat, comes in shaking water off her umbrella. She looks around the tank the way a diver might.

SAL
We're closed.

FRANCESCA
The door was open.

SAL
Doors do that.

She walks the length of the counter. She picks up a ukulele, plucks one string, puts it down.

FRANCESCA
Nice acoustics.

SAL
Used to be a tank for sharks. Now it's a tank for sharks.

FRANCESCA
(to herself)
Cute.

SAL
(to the room, though nobody else is there)
You hear that, boys? The lady thinks I'm cute.

FRANCESCA
Mr. DiMarco, a necklace went missing from the Hartwell estate on Tuesday. Emerald, platinum chain. It turned up on your ledger on Wednesday.

SAL
Ledger says a lot of things.

FRANCESCA
It says you paid four hundred dollars.

SAL
Charity.

FRANCESCA
It says you paid it to a boy named Teddy Hartwell. Age nine.

Sal sets down the bills. The neon buzzes louder.

SAL
Kids lie.

FRANCESCA
Teddy was telling the truth. He wanted to buy his mother a birthday present. He sold her own necklace to get it.

SAL
Then it's a sad story.

FRANCESCA
It's a sad story with a felony in it.

Sal slides one hand beneath the counter. Francesca's own hand goes to her coat.

SAL
You're brave, coming in here alone.

FRANCESCA
I'm not alone.

SAL
Who's with you?

She nods toward the cracked glass behind him. Through the algae, shapes move in the dark, a dozen uniforms.

SAL (CONT'D)
(a whisper, to himself)
Eel's finally out of the water.
